# Security

Configure a private reporting channel and a support policy before publication.
Never include credentials in public issues, URLs, logs or commits.

## Transport defaults

Only [[.Transport]] is enabled. Plaintext listeners require literal loopback IPs.
[[if .HTTP]]HTTP health checks are public. Application routes require explicitly reviewed
identity, authorization, input limits and safe errors; the CLI never mounts new APIs automatically.
[[end]][[if .GRPC]]All gRPC calls, including health probes, require a bearer token with at least 32
printable non-whitespace ASCII characters. Unary RPCs have bounded messages, deadlines
and panic recovery. Map application errors safely with infrastructure/rpc.Error.
Streaming methods need explicit lifetime and message policies. Gateway mappings do
not install HTTP authorization; review and mount them explicitly.
[[end]]Before external deployment add TLS and define the listener and caller identity policy.
Transport schemas never replace domain validation. Persistence, migrations and business
access policies are application-owned.

## Optional agents

Model endpoints come from trusted startup configuration, never request input; keys
are sent to those endpoints. Redirects, embedded URL credentials, queries and fragments
are rejected. No endpoint, history store or business tool is exposed automatically.
Authorize and validate all tools independently of model instructions. Preserve bounded
execution and context cancellation. Local fixtures do not prove provider acceptance.

## Source and contract tooling

CLI additions are offline and refuse overwrites. Respect .nostosx.lock ownership.
[[if .GRPC]]Contract commands execute pinned trusted Go/Buf tools and may access the network;
they are not a sandbox. Plugins must all succeed before outputs are replaced. Ordinary
publication failures attempt rollback; process termination can leave locks or staging
files. Review generated differences before building or deploying.
[[end]]
